 get the  breast off we're going to take the tip  of our knife and run it from the top of  the breastbone to the bottom and this is  going to help separate the breast meat  from the bone if you're worried about  getting it off in one piece honestly it  doesn't matter because we're gonna shred  it anyways and we're just trying to get  the meat away from the bones we can use  it to make stock you can see we have  some big chunks and we have our little  chunks we like to shred it into half  inch pieces so every bite has like the  perfect amount of chicken ratio to gravy  to veggies but you know what do you hear  if you want some chunks leave some  chunks once all your chicken is nicely  shredded just transfer it to an airtight  container and keep it in the fridge  until you're ready to use it now i'm  going to take this chicken carcass and  put it in our stock pot now it's time to  make our stock again we're doing it from  scratch it really just is the best way  to do it it will add so much flavor to  the filling and really enhance your dish  we tested it in a stove and a pressure  cooker there wasn't a difference in  taste but it was much faster in the  instant pot about a quarter of the time  it took on the stove so today we're  going to save some time and use the  pressure cooker if you don't have one  don't worry about it just simmer it on  your stove for four to six hours it will  still taste delicious i promise so to  the stock you're going to add onions  this one's literally a must don't skip  it garlic my personal favorite  carrots  celery peppercorns fresh thyme and  parsley and lastly a bay leaf now we're  just going to pour some water into our  pot for us this took about 12 to 16 cups  just make sure your chicken carcass is  covered by at least one inch of water  and that secret ingredient you cannot  forget add in those juices from roasting  your chicken if you're wondering where  the salt is i hear you but don't add  salt before you cook your stock you  ne