 mixture  together just until it comes together  you don't want it to be too wet and you  don't want it to be too dry it should  look just like this when you squeeze it  in your hand still nose too dry if it  starts to crumble when you roll it out  and if it's too wet it's going to be  like a sticky tacky dough and you just  want to avoid that dough can be  intimidating but it just takes practice  just keep going at it and you'll finally  get the rhythm of it great the dough  looks good it's awesome you might be  thinking this looks super dry don't  worry you saw when i squeezed it in my  hand it came together it's going to do  that exact same thing as we start to  form it gently shape this into a  rectangle then we're going to fold it in  thirds  turn it 90 degrees flatten it fold it in  thirds again this folding technique is  much like the lamination process you see  in croissants and biscuits that help you  get those extra flaky layers since team  two layer crust one our super official  tasty bowl let's divide our dough into  two  look at these layers those are the  layers you dream about at least i do  take your two dough babies wrap them  tightly in plastic stick them in the  fridge we recommend chilling your dough  for at least one hour but you can  actually do this the day before or even  up to three days let's talk filling to  me there is nothing worse than a dry pot  pie you need the golden ratio of veggies  to chicken to sauce so we're going to  start by melting the butter in a large  pot over medium heat we like to add  onions  carrots potatoes and fresh thyme you can  totally experiment with other vegetables  like parsnips or butternut squash or  leeks or beets but we went with more of  a classic version we're gonna season  these veggies with one teaspoon of  kosher salt and we're just gonna cook it  until the onions start to soften about  five minutes after it's softened we're  gonna add the flour and we wanna keep  stirring so it evenly incorporates into  the flour and there's no lumps this will  take like two to three minutes be  patient and do not turn that heat up if  you let the rube run too much you could  change the color of your filling or even  worse burn it and then you have to start  over  slowly whisk in the chicken stock a  little at a time you want to make sure  that the chicken stock is room  temperature so it evenly incorporates  into your roux if you try to add cold  liquid to hot roux it's going to seize  up and create a lumpy filling nobody  wants that so remember patience is key  let it simmer until the stock has  reduced into a nice thick sauce that  coats the back of your spoon  for a little silky lusciousness we like  to add just a touch of heavy cream once  it looks nice and thick give it one last  stir and turn off the heat then we're  going to fold in our beautifully  shredded chicken next we're going to add  in the frozen peas hear me out this is  the only frozen vegetable allowed in  this dish if you try to use that frozen  veggie mix you'll end up with a super  mushy filling with no texture we folded  in some parsley for a bit of brightness  it's just a nice contrast for a rich and  creamy dish like this so now take your  filling and chill it in the freezer for  15 minutes it's time to roll out the pie  dough let it sit on your counter for  five to ten minutes just so it's a bit  easier to roll out you're going to want  to flour your surface flour the rolling  pin and flour your dough then gently  roll out the dough give it a quarter  turn  keep rolling and keep repeating it's  okay if it doesn't look perfect we're  going to trim the edges later both of  our doughs rolled out to about a 12 inch  circle we want the bottom one just a  little bit thicker so it's sturdy enough  to hold our filling the best way to move  your pie dough is really just roll it up  on your rolling pin then it's time to  put it in your pie dish we like to use a  glass pie dish it heats evenly and you  can see what's happening metal conducts  heat a bit better but it might leave you  with a burnt crust and we don't want  that so drip your dough over the pie  dish let it naturally find its place and  then you can make any adjustments press  down the sides and make sure there's no  air bubbles and make sure it's really  deep down touching all the sides of the  dish before you add your filling we have  another trick egg wash you just want to  brush the inside of your crust with some  of it it's going to act as a protective  layer and prevent you from getting a  soggy crust now that your crust is in  you're gonna add your chilled filling  just even it out so it's in a nice even  layer gently drape your remaining pie  dough over the pie trim off any excess  then pinch the edges of the crust  together roll it under and seal it  together then crimp it around the sides  just like this all right let's go with  the egg wash we want a nice even layer  oops  it's a little chunky i'm just going to  smooth this out nobody saw anything and  we're just going to cut a few vents in  the top to allow the steam to escape  make sure you do this after your egg  wash because if you egg washed now you  would just be sealing it back together  your pie is fully assembled you are  almost done can we just take a minute to  bask in this beauty all that hard work  is totally paying off but we just have  one more step we're gonna put this in  the fridge just for about 20 minutes so  it holds its shape when it bakes you  definitely don't want that butter to  melt before you bake it the grand finale  is here it is time to bake this bad boy  we like to bake our pie on the lower  third of the oven which helps to  concentrate the heat on the bottom to  ensure that crispy crust then we're  going to bake it for about 40 to 50  minutes rotating halfway through and  just like that you're done you have this  beautiful golden brown crust your  filling is bubbling and juicy it's  incredible but wait i lied i lied yo